WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.840
Tick bites are common if you spend time in grassy or wooded areas,

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:07.760
and the most important thing to remember is that early removal is the best way to

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:11.200
reduce your risk of infection. While a bite might just look like a

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:15.200
small red bump at first, ticks can transmit serious illnesses like

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:18.480
Lyme disease. When it comes to removing a tick, please

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:21.440
ignore those old home remedies like using heat,

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:26.719
nail polish, or petroleum jelly. Instead, use fine-tipped tweezers to grasp the

00:00:26.719 --> 00:00:29.840
tick as close to the skin's surface as you can.

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:33.680
Pull upward with steady, even pressure without twisting or jerking,

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:37.520
which helps ensure you remove the entire tick. Once it's out,

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:40.959
wash the site with soap and water and apply an antiseptic.

00:00:40.959 --> 00:00:44.799
It is recommended to place the tick in a sealed bag or container

00:00:44.799 --> 00:00:48.959
labeled with the date, time, and where on the body it was found.

00:00:48.959 --> 00:00:52.480
This is incredibly helpful for doctors or public health officials

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:57.119
if you need a medical evaluation later, as they can identify the species or even

00:00:57.119 --> 00:01:00.240
test the tick itself to decide on the best treatment.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:03.520
Finally, keep a close watch on the area for several weeks.

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:07.919
If you notice a bullseye rash or if the person develops flu-like symptoms,

00:01:07.919 --> 00:01:12.480
a fever, or joint pain, they should see a health care provider right away and be

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:16.080
sure to tell them about the tick bite.

